WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.385
Assessment Alignment and Rubrics.

2
00:00:03.385 --> 00:00:08.822
ID 553 - Module 4: Objectives and Aligned Assessment.

3
00:00:08.822 --> 00:00:14.977
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.577 --> 00:00:23.808
In Dick and Carey, assessment instruments are developed
immediately after objectives and before instructional
strategies.

5
00:00:23.808 --> 00:00:30.543
Backward Design makes the same move: decide what evidence
you'll accept before you plan activities.

6
00:00:30.543 --> 00:00:32.175
The reason is alignment.

7
00:00:32.175 --> 00:00:44.420
If the assessment is designed last, it measures what was
convenient to teach rather than what the objective required,
and learners learn to the test rather than to the
performance.

8
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:53.740
An assessment is aligned when the performance it elicits,
the conditions it imposes, and the standard it applies all
match the objective.

9
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.644
Check all three, every time.

10
00:00:56.244 --> 00:01:07.250
Assessments serve different purposes at different times:
entry-skill tests, pretests, embedded practice checks,
posttests, and performance assessments.

11
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:15.924
Every project needs the last two, and most benefit from
embedded checks, because retrieval practice improves
retention.

12
00:01:15.924 --> 00:01:18.621
All of them are criterion-referenced.

13
00:01:18.621 --> 00:01:25.254
Each learner is measured against the standard in the
objective, not against other learners.

14
00:01:25.254 --> 00:01:32.834
Grading on a curve makes no sense when the question is
whether a nurse can administer medication safely.

15
00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:38.957
If learners fail in large numbers, the instruction is the
problem, not the learners.

16
00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:47.145
Take one objective: given a mismatch alert, the nurse will
decide whether to stop and state the reason.

17
00:01:47.145 --> 00:01:49.060
Three aligned assessments.

18
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:56.942
First, six new alert scenarios the learner hasn't seen,
deciding in each whether to stop, with a rationale.

19
00:01:56.942 --> 00:02:03.720
Second, a branching simulation in the practice environment
that throws alerts at two points.

20
00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.393
Third, observed performance on a checklist during a
simulated administration.

21
00:02:09.393 --> 00:02:20.074
All three present a decision, because the objective is a
decision, and all three use new situations rather than the
examples used in instruction.

22
00:02:20.674 --> 00:02:24.201
Now three misaligned items for the same objective.

23
00:02:24.201 --> 00:02:27.799
'Define mismatch alert' tests recall, not decision.

24
00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:33.794
'List the three alert types' tests verbal information when
the objective is rule use.

25
00:02:33.794 --> 00:02:43.952
'Describe the alert workflow in your own words' asks for
description, and describing a workflow is not the same as
performing it under pressure.

26
00:02:43.952 --> 00:02:51.711
The most common alignment error in the field is exactly
this: testing a rule-use objective with a recall item.

27
00:02:51.711 --> 00:02:56.578
If the objective says decide, the assessment must present a
decision.

28
00:02:57.178 --> 00:03:00.163
Match the item type to the outcome type.

29
00:03:00.163 --> 00:03:05.313
Verbal information suits short answer, matching, and
multiple choice.

30
00:03:05.313 --> 00:03:12.179
Concepts need classification of new examples and non-
examples, not recall of the definition.

31
00:03:12.179 --> 00:03:18.298
Rules and problem solving need new problems, scenarios, and
cases with a decision.

32
00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:25.761
Motor skills need observed performance with a checklist, not
a written description of the procedure.

33
00:03:25.761 --> 00:03:34.119
Attitudes need observed choices in realistic situations; a
quiz on what the right attitude is tells you nothing.

34
00:03:34.119 --> 00:03:38.821
And create-level objectives need products judged with a
rubric.

35
00:03:39.421 --> 00:03:43.141
When you do write multiple choice, follow the rules.

36
00:03:43.141 --> 00:03:49.650
The stem states a complete problem, so the learner could
answer before reading the options.

37
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:55.731
Options are parallel in length and grammar, and the correct
answer isn't the longest.

38
00:03:55.731 --> 00:04:05.888
Distractors are plausible and, ideally, drawn from the
common errors you recorded in the task analysis; a
distractor nobody chooses is wasted.

39
00:04:05.888 --> 00:04:09.536
Avoid 'all of the above' and negatives in the stem.

40
00:04:09.536 --> 00:04:15.974
And tag each item with its objective number so you can
analyze results by objective later.

41
00:04:16.574 --> 00:04:22.562
For performance and product objectives, the assessment is a
task plus a scoring guide.

42
00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:26.113
Checklists suit procedures with a correct sequence.

43
00:04:26.113 --> 00:04:29.734
Rating scales are fast but unreliable across raters.

44
00:04:29.734 --> 00:04:39.063
Analytic rubrics, which describe each criterion at each
level of performance, are the most reliable and give the
most useful feedback.

45
00:04:39.063 --> 00:05:02.527
Build one in five steps: list three to six criteria from the
objective and task analysis; describe the exemplary level in
observable terms; describe the beginning level; fill in the
middle by naming what's present and what's missing; then
test the rubric with a second rater on two or three real
samples and revise wherever you disagree.

46
00:05:02.527 --> 00:05:06.566
Every assignment rubric in this course was built that way.

47
00:05:07.166 --> 00:05:08.398
Three last things.

48
00:05:08.398 --> 00:05:20.581
Assessment during instruction is only formative if learners
get feedback they can use: specific to the criterion,
telling them what to do next, arriving while they can still
act.

49
00:05:20.581 --> 00:05:26.468
Validity means the assessment measures the objective, and
alignment is the main check.

50
00:05:26.468 --> 00:05:35.845
Reliability means consistent results across items,
occasions, and raters; enough items, clear rubrics, and
rater training are the levers.

51
00:05:35.845 --> 00:05:47.139
Fairness means the assessment doesn't disadvantage learners
for reasons unrelated to the objective, so offer multiple
means of expression where the objective allows.

52
00:05:47.139 --> 00:05:54.737
Draft the assessment for your terminal objective now and
bring it to the objective clinic with your objectives.
